IMPORT prelude

-- Sale of Goods Act 1979 (Singapore) -- what a buyer of goods can rely on: void contracts for perished goods (s 6), the implied
-- conditions of satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose (s 14, with the slight-breach rule of s 15A), title (ss 21, 23), when
-- property and risk pass on approval or sale or return (ss 18 Rule 4, 20), wrong quantities (s 30), acceptance and the loss of the
-- right to reject (ss 11(3), 35), damages (ss 50, 51, 53) and auctions (s 57).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SGA1979.txt. The deposit's cover says the revised
-- edition "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021" and came into operation on 31 December 2021; its page
-- furniture reads "Informal Consolidation - version in force from 1/4/2022". The latest amendment annotated is Act 25 of 2021
-- wef 01/04/2022 (ss 52 and 61, "claimant").
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: every purchase of goods in Singapore, from a hawker stall to a car, is a
-- contract of sale under it, and it is where a buyer's right to goods of satisfactory quality comes from. SCOPED to the provisions
-- an individual buyer most often meets. Not encoded: formation and price (ss 2-5, 7-9), sale by description and sample (ss 13, 15)
-- beyond their mention in s 15A, ascertainment and bulk goods (ss 16, 17, 18 Rules 1-3 and 5, 19, 20A, 20B), sellers and buyers in
-- possession (ss 24-26), delivery rules (ss 27-29, 31-34), partial rejection (s 35A), the unpaid seller's lien, stoppage and
-- re-sale (Part 5), specific performance (s 52), exclusion of implied terms (s 55, which defers to the Unfair Contract Terms Act
-- 1977, not deposited here), and the Schedule's modifications for contracts made before 1985.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the conventions of the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `Sale of Goods Act 1979 -- buyers`

§§ `6 -- goods that had already perished`

-- s 6: "Where there is a contract for the sale of specific goods, and the goods without the knowledge of the seller have perished
-- at the time when the contract is made, the contract is void." The Act says nothing here about a seller who knew; this rule
-- returns FALSE for that case only because s 6 does not reach it.
GIVEN `the goods are specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN, `the goods had perished when the contract was made` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the seller knew they had perished` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the contract is void under section 6` IF
        `the goods are specific goods`
    AND `the goods had perished when the contract was made`
    AND (NOT `the seller knew they had perished`)

§§ `14, 15A -- satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose`

-- s 14(1): "there is no implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness" except as this section and s 15 provide.
-- s 14(2): "Where the seller sells goods in the course of a business, there is an implied condition that the goods supplied under
-- the contract are of satisfactory quality." (2A) the standard "a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory".
-- s 14(2C): the condition "does not extend to any matter" (a) "specifically drawn to the buyer's attention before the contract is
-- made"; (b) "where the buyer examines the goods before the contract is made, which that examination ought to reveal".
-- s 14(3): where the buyer "makes known ... any particular purpose", the goods must be "reasonably fit for that purpose", "except
-- where the circumstances show that the buyer does not rely, or that it is unreasonable for him to rely, on the skill or judgment
-- of the seller".
-- s 14(5): these apply to a sale by "a person who in the course of a business is acting as agent for another", "except where that
-- other is not selling in the course of a business and either the buyer knows that fact or reasonable steps are taken to bring it
-- to the notice of the buyer".
-- s 15A(1): where the breach of a condition implied by s 13, 14 or 15 "is so slight that it would be unreasonable for the buyer to
-- reject them, then, if the buyer does not deal as consumer, the breach is not to be treated as a breach of condition but may be
-- treated as a breach of warranty". (3) "It is for the seller to show" the breach is slight. s 61(4B): "it is for a seller
-- claiming that the buyer does not deal as consumer to show that he does not."
-- s 11(3): once a buyer under a non-severable contract "has accepted the goods or part of them", breach of a condition "can only be
-- treated as a breach of warranty". s 53(1): for a breach of warranty the buyer may not reject but may set it off against the price
-- or "maintain an action ... for damages".
DECLARE Purchase HAS
    `the seller sells in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a business agent sells for a private owner` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer knew or was told the owner was private` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defect was drawn to the buyer's attention before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer examined the goods before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `that examination ought to have revealed the defect` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer made a particular purpose known` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer did not or could not reasonably rely on the seller` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has shown the buyer does not deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has shown the breach is so slight that rejection is unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer has accepted the goods` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the purchase` IS A Purchase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the seller is treated as selling in the course of a business` IF
        `the purchase`'s `the seller sells in the course of a business`
    OR  (    `the purchase`'s `a business agent sells for a private owner`
         AND (NOT `the purchase`'s `the buyer knew or was told the owner was private`))

GIVEN `the purchase` IS A Purchase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the satisfactory quality condition covers the defect` IF
        `the seller is treated as selling in the course of a business` `the purchase`
    AND (NOT `the purchase`'s `the defect was drawn to the buyer's attention before the contract`)
    AND (NOT (    `the purchase`'s `the buyer examined the goods before the contract`
              AND `the purchase`'s `that examination ought to have revealed the defect`))

GIVEN `the purchase` IS A Purchase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fitness for purpose condition is implied` IF
        `the seller is treated as selling in the course of a business` `the purchase`
    AND `the purchase`'s `the buyer made a particular purpose known`
    AND (NOT `the purchase`'s `the buyer did not or could not reasonably rely on the seller`)

-- Assumes the contract is not severable (s 11(3)) and ignores partial rejection (s 35A).
GIVEN `the purchase` IS A Purchase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may reject the goods for the defect` IF
        `the satisfactory quality condition covers the defect` `the purchase`
    AND (NOT `the purchase`'s `the buyer has accepted the goods`)
    AND (NOT (    `the purchase`'s `the seller has shown the buyer does not deal as consumer`
              AND `the purchase`'s `the seller has shown the breach is so slight that rejection is unreasonable`))

GIVEN `the purchase` IS A Purchase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may claim damages for the defect` IF
    `the satisfactory quality condition covers the defect` `the purchase`

§§ `35 -- acceptance`

-- s 35(1): the buyer is deemed to have accepted "(a) when he intimates to the seller that he has accepted them; or (b) when the
-- goods have been delivered to him and he does any act in relation to them which is inconsistent with the ownership of the seller".
-- (2): a buyer who has not previously examined them "is not deemed to have accepted them under subsection (1) until he has had a
-- reasonable opportunity of examining them". (3) a consumer "cannot lose his right to rely on subsection (2) by agreement, waiver or
-- otherwise". (4): also "when after the lapse of a reasonable time he retains the goods without intimating to the seller that he
-- has rejected them". (6): NOT "merely because (a) he asks for, or agrees to, their repair".
-- The repair flag is deliberately inert: s 35(6) makes it no ground of acceptance. Assumes the goods have been delivered.
GIVEN `told the seller it accepted` IS A BOOLEAN, `did an act inconsistent with the seller's ownership` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `had examined or had a reasonable opportunity to examine` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `kept the goods beyond a reasonable time without rejecting` IS A BOOLEAN, `asked the seller to repair them` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer is deemed to have accepted` IF
        (    (`told the seller it accepted` OR `did an act inconsistent with the seller's ownership`)
         AND `had examined or had a reasonable opportunity to examine`)
    OR  `kept the goods beyond a reasonable time without rejecting`

§§ `30 -- the wrong quantity`

-- s 30(1): if the seller delivers less, "the buyer may reject them, but if the buyer accepts the goods so delivered, he must pay for
-- them at the contract rate". (2): if more, the buyer may accept the contract goods "and reject the rest, or he may reject the
-- whole". (2A) [4/2014]: a buyer "who does not deal as consumer" may not reject under (1) or reject the whole under (2) "if the
-- shortfall or, as the case may be, excess is so slight that it would be unreasonable". (2B) "It is for the seller to show" that.
-- (3): accepting the whole of a larger delivery, "he must pay for them at the contract rate".
GIVEN `units delivered` IS A NUMBER, `units contracted` IS A NUMBER,
      `the seller has shown the buyer is not a consumer` IS A BOOLEAN, `the seller has shown the difference is slight` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may reject the whole delivery` IF
    IF `units delivered` EQUALS `units contracted`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE NOT (`the seller has shown the buyer is not a consumer` AND `the seller has shown the difference is slight`)

GIVEN `units accepted` IS A NUMBER, `the contract rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the price for the units accepted` IS `units accepted` TIMES `the contract rate`

§§ `21, 23 -- title`

-- s 21(1): where goods are sold by a non-owner without the owner's authority or consent, "the buyer acquires no better title to the
-- goods than the seller had, unless the owner of the goods is by his conduct precluded from denying the seller's authority to sell".
-- s 23: where the seller "has a voidable title to them, but his title has not been avoided at the time of the sale, the buyer
-- acquires a good title to the goods, provided he buys them in good faith and without notice of the seller's defect of title".
-- s 61(2): good faith means "in fact done honestly, whether it is done negligently or not". The exceptions in ss 24, 25 and the
-- Factors Act 1889 (s 21(2)) are not encoded.
GIVEN `the seller owned the goods or sold with the owner's authority` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the owner's conduct precludes denying the seller's authority` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the seller had a voidable title not yet avoided` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the buyer bought honestly without notice of the defect in title` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer acquires a good title` IF
        `the seller owned the goods or sold with the owner's authority`
    OR  `the owner's conduct precludes denying the seller's authority`
    OR  (`the seller had a voidable title not yet avoided` AND `the buyer bought honestly without notice of the defect in title`)

§§ `18 Rule 4, 20 -- goods on approval, and risk`

-- s 18 Rule 4: goods delivered "on approval or on sale or return" pass to the buyer "(i) when he signifies his approval or
-- acceptance to the seller or does any other act adopting the transaction; (ii) if he ... retains the goods without giving notice
-- of rejection", on expiry of the time fixed for return or, if none, "a reasonable time".
-- s 20(1): "Unless otherwise agreed, the goods remain at the seller's risk until the property in them is transferred to the buyer",
-- then they are at the buyer's risk "whether delivery has been made or not". (2): where delivery is delayed through a party's fault,
-- "the goods are at the risk of the party at fault as regards any loss which might not have occurred but for such fault".
-- Agreement otherwise is not modelled.
GIVEN `signified approval or adopted the transaction` IS A BOOLEAN, `kept the goods without notice of rejection` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the fixed or a reasonable time for return has expired` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `property passes under Rule 4` IF
        `signified approval or adopted the transaction`
    OR  (`kept the goods without notice of rejection` AND `the fixed or a reasonable time for return has expired`)

GIVEN `property has passed to the buyer` IS A BOOLEAN, `the loss is due to delivery delayed by the buyer's fault` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the loss is due to delivery delayed by the seller's fault` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer bears the loss` IF
        `the loss is due to delivery delayed by the buyer's fault`
    OR  (`property has passed to the buyer` AND (NOT `the loss is due to delivery delayed by the seller's fault`))

§§ `50, 51, 53 -- damages`

-- s 53(3): "In the case of breach of warranty of quality, such loss is prima facie the difference between the value of the goods at
-- the time of delivery to the buyer and the value they would have had if they had fulfilled the warranty."
-- s 51(3) (non-delivery) and s 50(3) (non-acceptance): where there is an available market, prima facie "the difference between the
-- contract price and the market or current price". The text does not say which way round; the direction and the floor at zero
-- below are INFERENCES from the measure in ss 50(2), 51(2), "the estimated loss directly and naturally resulting".
GIVEN `value as warranted` IS A NUMBER, `value as delivered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the prima facie damages for breach of warranty of quality` IS
    IF `value as warranted` GREATER THAN `value as delivered` THEN `value as warranted` MINUS `value as delivered` ELSE 0

GIVEN `the contract price` IS A NUMBER, `the market price` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the prima facie damages for non-delivery` IS
    IF `the market price` GREATER THAN `the contract price` THEN `the market price` MINUS `the contract price` ELSE 0

GIVEN `the contract price` IS A NUMBER, `the market price` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the prima facie damages for non-acceptance` IS
    IF `the contract price` GREATER THAN `the market price` THEN `the contract price` MINUS `the market price` ELSE 0

§§ `57 -- auctions`

-- s 57(2): a sale by auction "is complete when the auctioneer announces its completion by the fall of the hammer ... and until the
-- announcement is made any bidder may retract his bid". (4): where the sale "is not notified to be subject to a right to bid by or
-- on behalf of the seller, it is not lawful for the seller to bid himself or to employ any person to bid". (5): such a sale "may be
-- treated as fraudulent by the buyer". (6): where a right to bid "is expressly reserved (but not otherwise)" the seller or one
-- person on his behalf may bid.
GIVEN `the hammer has fallen` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a bidder may retract the bid` IF NOT `the hammer has fallen`

GIVEN `the seller or someone employed by the seller bid` IS A BOOLEAN, `a right to bid for the seller was notified` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may treat the auction sale as fraudulent` IF
    `the seller or someone employed by the seller bid` AND (NOT `a right to bid for the seller was notified`)
